# Layered Architecture (Java / Spring)

Default synchronous flow:

```text
Client -> Controller (implements generated api) -> Service -> Manager / Domain Logic -> Repository / DAO -> Database
```

External integration: `Service / Manager -> Client / Adapter -> External System`

Not every feature needs every layer. Do not create layers just because the pattern exists.

## Controller
Responsible for HTTP concerns only: implements generated OpenAPI interfaces, basic validation glue, calling a Service, HTTP status codes.

Never in controllers: business logic, DB queries, direct repository calls, complex object construction, long workflows, external API orchestration.

## Service
Represents an application use case: coordinate the flow, application-level validation, transaction boundaries, call repositories and managers, trigger events, coordinate integrations.
Express *what the application is doing*, not low-level infrastructure. Avoid huge services that hold every capability.

## Manager
A reusable business/domain capability or complex orchestration (e.g. EnrichmentManager).
- Service = use-case orchestration. Manager = reusable business capability.
- Do NOT create a Manager just because a feature "should have one". If a Service is simple, keep the logic there.

## Repository / DAO
Persistence only: queries, CRUD, filtering, pagination, sorting, efficient retrieval.
- Never access the DB directly from controllers.

## Entity / Domain / Api / Contract
- **entity**: persistence only (JPA).
- **domain**: business meaning inside analytics (hand-written).
- **api**: generated HTTP models/interfaces for one service.
- **contract**: generated shared wire models for Kafka + ingest body.
- Never return entities from controllers. Never put JPA on api/contract models. Never put score labels in contracts.

## Mapper
MapStruct mappers in `mapper/` convert contract/api ↔ entity/domain. No business logic in mappers.

## Dependency Injection
- Constructor injection with `final` fields. Prefer `@RequiredArgsConstructor` when the constructor only wires collaborators (no `@Value` params).
- Keep an explicit constructor when any parameter needs `@Value` (or other non-Lombok annotations) — put `@Value` on the **constructor parameter**, not on a mutable field.
- Spring tests: `@Autowired` / `@MockBean` on the test class is fine for MockMvc E2E; production code never uses field `@Autowired`.
- Avoid: manual construction of dependencies in business classes, static global state, service locators, `ReflectionTestUtils` for config that belongs in the constructor.

## Package Structure (baseline)
```text
api/ (generated)  controller/  service/  manager/  domain/  repository/  entity/
mapper/  client/  exception/  config/  messaging/{producer,consumer}/  web/
```
Do not add a hand-written `dto/` package. If the project already uses a coherent structure, follow it—except `dto/` which is deprecated.
